<div class="start-card"><p><strong>You will learn:</strong> how to provision dependent Azure resources in understandable stages and prove model access before starting a cluster.</p><p><strong>Starting requirements:</strong> the prerequisite checker passes; the selected subscription, region and source IP are correct.</p><p><strong>Shell and directory:</strong> Bash, repository root. Keep the virtual environment available for later verification tools.</p></div>
<h2 id="cost">Read the cost boundary first</h2>
<p>This is a <strong>paid workshop</strong>. A planning estimate checked on <strong>11 November 2026</strong> is roughly <strong>US$5–15 for one four-hour walkthrough</strong> with the small sample and prompt cleanup, before tax, discounts and subscription-specific rates. This is an allowance, not a measured bill or a guaranteed price. Resources left running for a day can cost materially more.</p>
<p>Main drivers are the AKS VM and disk, PostgreSQL compute/storage, NAT gateway and public IPs, Standard Service Bus/App Configuration, model tokens, and any diagnostic ingestion. AKS's Free management tier does not make its VM free. GlobalStandard model processing may occur outside the account's region. Check the <a href="https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/calculator/">Azure calculator</a> and your subscription's actual offers; the <a href="./reference.html#costs">reference page lists cost drivers</a>. You will deliberately confirm the group name before the first creation.</p>
<h2 id="stages">Start the first two stages</h2>
{{snippet:commands.sh:provision}}
<h3>Stage 01: models before machines</h3>
<p>{{file:scripts/01-models.sh}} checks prerequisites again, creates a tagged workshop-only resource group, and deploys {{file:infra/models.bicep}}. It creates an Azure OpenAI resource with key authentication disabled, a chat deployment named <code>chat</code>, and an embedding deployment named <code>embedding</code>. Versions, SKUs and requested capacities come from the bootstrap configuration.</p>
<p>The default chat model is <code>gpt-5-mini</code>, version <code>2025-08-07</code>; the embedding model is <code>text-embedding-3-small</code>, version <code>1</code>. {{file:tools/model_probe.py}} obtains an Entra token, requests one 1536-dimensional embedding, and checks for a non-empty chat response. RBAC propagation retries are bounded. Model tokens are billed even during this small test.</p>
<p>If that stage fails, no AKS or database provisioning has begun. Fix model access, regional deployment support or quota first. The tool does not silently substitute a different model or change vector dimensions.</p>
<h3>Stage 02: foundation, then independent long operations</h3>
<p>{{file:infra/foundation.bicep}} creates a virtual network with separate AKS, Functions and PostgreSQL subnets; a NAT gateway with a static egress IP; private PostgreSQL DNS; ACR; a storage account and private containers; request/reply queues; Key Vault; App Configuration; five managed identities; and Function telemetry. It explicitly provides outbound connectivity instead of relying on implicit default outbound access.</p>
<p>After the foundation is ready, the script creates random PostgreSQL and browser passwords directly in Key Vault. Values are not printed. The PostgreSQL secure ARM parameter is written to a permission-restricted temporary file only while submitting that deployment, then deleted. Never enable shell tracing with <code>set -x</code> around these scripts.</p>
<p>{{file:infra/database.bicep}} creates PostgreSQL 17 Flexible Server on the delegated private subnet, a database called <code>products</code>, and the <code>VECTOR</code> extension allowlist setting. {{file:infra/compute.bicep}} independently starts one-node AKS with workload identity, OIDC and the KEDA add-on, and a Python 3.12 Flex Consumption Function app integrated into its delegated subnet.</p>
<h2 id="network">Know where traffic can go</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Connection</th><th>Core choice</th><th>Why it works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AKS / Function → PostgreSQL</td><td>Private VNet address and private DNS</td><td>All three dedicated subnets share the VNet. No public database firewall opening is needed.</td></tr><tr><td>Workloads → Storage, queues, models, settings, vault</td><td>Public HTTPS/TLS service endpoints + Entra identity</td><td>NAT provides explicit egress. “Public endpoint” does not mean anonymous access.</td></tr><tr><td>Event Grid → Blob extension webhook</td><td>Function's public HTTPS endpoint + Blob-extension system key</td><td>Registered after code publication; only the manuals container's BlobCreated events match.</td></tr><tr><td>Browser → Products</td><td>Public load balancer restricted to your IPv4 /32; self-signed TLS + sign-in</td><td>Certificate setup happens after the load balancer has an IP. Blob contents remain private.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2 id="while-waiting">Use the provisioning time</h2>
<p>The command returns after ARM accepts the two long deployments; it does not claim they are ready. Now continue directly to the Docker chapter. Do not run later dependent stages until <code>03-ready.sh</code> confirms both operations. Resume state and non-secret outputs live under <code>workshop/.state/</code>. A different terminal still uses the same subscription and group through <code>common.sh</code>.</p>
<div class="verification"><strong>Verification:</strong> expect the final line to say that AKS/Functions and PostgreSQL are provisioning. This is example/expected behavior. Stage 03 will poll actual deployment states, report failed ARM operations and enforce a 40-minute deadline for each operation.</div>
<h3>Common failures</h3><ul><li><strong>A name is already soft-deleted:</strong> inspect the matching Key Vault/App Configuration name. Recover your own workshop resource deliberately, or use a new prefix in a new state folder. Do not purge or adopt someone else's resource.</li><li><strong>Resource policy or region denies an SKU:</strong> read the failed ARM operation. Choose a compatible region/SKU in the bootstrap configuration before a new deployment, or have the owner adjust policy. A successful model probe does not grant compute quota.</li><li><strong>A rerun finds an existing resource group:</strong> the script verifies its <code>workshop-id</code> tag. It refuses to modify a group with a different tag; never work around that by changing another group's tags.</li><li><strong>Interrupted stage:</strong> rerun the same stage. Names, role-assignment IDs and secret reuse are deliberate. Check the deployment state before replacing configuration.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Continue now:</strong> read and build Products while provisioning runs. You do not need to wait in this chapter.</p>
